  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SP&amp;LR for WWs in ASEAN Agri-food Sector</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The overall project goal is to ensure women working in the  agri-food sector in Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam enjoy the right to decent work, social protection and protection against violence and discrimination.

This initiative aims to address the asymmetries in the policy making and social architecture that deny agri-food sector workers, many of whom are wage workers working in informal conditions, a dignified and decent work environment. It will contribute to recognition and valuation of agri-food sector workers who represent an important share of the informal economy and comprises a large sector such as small-scale farmers and food producers, food and street vendors, plantations and food processing workers. The project will have a regional scope in ASEAN while the country-level work will focus on Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam addressing gaps in legislation that allow room to capitalise on the ambiguities of existing legal definitions and aiming to overcome informal workers&#x2019; limited opportunities for the right to decent work, social protection, collective bargaining and every right entitlement like other formal sector workers.
The project aims to build strong connections to the grassroots constituencies and focuses on workers mobilisation. Stronger articulation on policy influencing at the national level and engagement with the national machinery, national human rights institutions and other relevant stakeholders could make the policy change process more convincing.

The project will forefront the narrative and leadership of agri-food sector women workers by situating women workers&#x2019; struggles within a broader women&#x2019;s movement and by amplifying their demand for decent work and social protection, thus making a case for gender equality in the sector and seeking to strengthen institutional response.

The initiative will beguided by our ideological framework of women&#x2019;s economic rights and justice based on the universal nature of human rights and the principles of substantive equality and no discrimination which recognises women&#x2019;s agency and has potential for structural change.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="23">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">IWRAW is a feminist organisation committed to the full realisation of women&apos;s human rights through the pursuit of equality. Established on 05 March 1996. The political intent of IWRAW Asia Pacific is to disrupt right-wing populism and extremism, corporate and state capture by organising, mobilising and building political knowledge and analysis with women&#x2019;s groups to demand accountability fromState and non-State actors in respect of gender equality and women&#x2019;s human rights.

Vision: a world in which everyone enjoys human rights and fundamental freedoms on the basis of equality, without discrimination on the grounds of sex or gender, and free of oppressive power relationships, with individuals and societies benefiting from sustainable and inclusive development.

Mission: To achieveits vision, IWRAW adopts an approach based on the universal nature of human rights, focusing particularly on the experience of women and girls from the Global South and recognising the need to eliminate multiple and intersecting forms of discrimination at all levels. Working in collaboration with partners and through alliances with others, it aims to act as catalysts in building capacity for change and for enhancing the realisation of substantial equality and women&#x2019;s human rights</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In&#x2019;t Women Rights ActionWatch Asia</narrative>
